                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Project Motor Racing is a great game for anyone looking to ease their way into sim racing. The driving experience is enjoyable, and the game itself is genuinely decent, especially if you're coming from more casual racing games.\n\nOne of my favourite features is the career mode. I love the idea of building your own racing career, but I feel there's a lot more potential when it comes to personalisation and progression.\n\nCareer Mode Improvements I'd Love to See:\n\nCustom Racing Numbers: Allow players to choose their own racing number to make their car feel more personal.\n\nSponsorship Customisation: Let players customise the sponsors on their car's livery to reflect the sponsorship deals they've actually signed in career mode.\n\nReputation and Fame System: Introduce a progression system where your performance on track influences your reputation, attracting bigger sponsors, better contracts and opportunities with more prestigious teams. It would be great to start with smaller sponsorship deals and gradually unlock bigger opportunities as your career develops.\n\nMore GT3 Cars: I'd particularly love to see the Ferrari 296 GT3 added to the game. Having Ferrari represented in the GT3 category would be a fantastic addition and bring even more variety to the grid.\n\nGraphics and Visual Improvements:\n\nThe graphics aren't the best compared to some other modern racing simulators, and there are definitely areas that could use improvement. That being said, I don't think they're terrible, and there's plenty of room for the developers to improve the visuals through future updates.\n\nVehicle Sounds Need Some Attention:\n\nOne area that I feel really needs improvement is the vehicle audio, particularly the difference between onboard and external trackside cameras.\n\nFor example, the Lamborghini Huracán GT3 sounds fantastic when driving from inside the cockpit or using the onboard cameras. The engine has that loud, aggressive, deep growl you'd expect from a Lamborghini GT3 car.\n\nHowever, when switching to the external cameras positioned around the circuit, the sound changes dramatically. Instead of hearing that powerful, aggressive engine note, the car sounds strangely high-pitched, almost like an electric vehicle.\n\nThis takes away from the immersion, especially when watching replays or using the broadcast-style cameras. I'd love to see the developers improve the external engine audio so that the cars retain their distinctive engine characteristics, while still realistically accounting for distance and the Doppler effect.\n\nA GT3 car should sound just as exciting flying past a trackside camera as it does from inside the cockpit.\n\nOverall Thoughts:\n\nDespite these criticisms, I'm genuinely enjoying Project Motor Racing. It's a solid introduction to sim racing, and the career mode is one of the main reasons I've been enjoying it so much.\n\nThe game has a good foundation, but I feel it has the potential to become something much better with continued development.\n\nMore career customisation, a proper sponsorship and reputation progression system, additional GT3 cars such as the Ferrari 296 GT3, improved graphics and more authentic external vehicle sounds would all make a massive difference.\n\nI'd recommend the game, especially to anyone looking to get into sim racing, but I hope the developers continue improving it because there's definitely a lot of untapped potential here.",
